.TH WERKMEISTER-INFRA-CONNECT 1 "" "werkmeister" "Werkmeister Manual"
.SH NAME
werkmeister\-infra\-connect \- Start all shared infrastructure (local + k8s port\-forwards)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBwerkmeister infra connect\fR
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-k8s\fR
Start/restart k8s port\-forwards container only (replaces standalone k8s\-connect command)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fB`\-\-k8s`\fR starts or checks only the containerized k8s port\-forwards. Starts the \fBk8s\-forwards\fR Docker container (part of \fBinfra/compose/shared\-infra.yml\fR), which runs one \fBkubectl port\-forward\fR process per entry in the generated forwards table, all inside a single container. If the container is already healthy, the command says so and does nothing.
.PP
The container runs on the \fBwerkmeister\-shared\fR Docker network and is accessible to other containers via the DNS name \fBk8s\-forwards\fR. Port\-forward processes inside the container auto\-restart if they disconnect, managed by the container's entrypoint script.
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
=== k8s Connect ===

[info] Attempting to start/restart k8s\-forwards container...
[ok]   k8s port\-forwards started
.RE
.fi
.PP
If the forwards can't be started, it prints \fBFailed to start k8s port\-forwards.\fR and points at Docker and your kubeconfig. \fBwerkmeister infra status\fR lists each forward and whether it is alive.
.PP
Without \fB\-\-k8s\fR, it starts all shared infrastructure, waits up to 90 seconds for it to settle, then prints a health summary (\fBcaddy\fR, \fBk8s\-forwards\fR and any opted\-in tools), the issue\-tracker check, and the developer\-tool links:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
=== Werkmeister Connect ===

\-\-\- Health Summary \-\-\-

  caddy                          healthy
  k8s\-forwards                   healthy

  All 2 services healthy
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBwerkmeister\fR(1)
